<Application xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.App">

  <!--
    Four style layers, in this order:

      1. FluentTheme        — whatever layer 2 has NOT templated, which since Round 7 Task 9 means:
                              the PathIcon this app's spinner and combo chevrons are drawn with, the
                              MenuFlyout presenter every text box's context menu is drawn in,
                              MessageBoxWindow's own Window chrome (MainWindow's caption is
                              hand-drawn since Round 7 Task 1 and is therefore NOT Fluent's any
                              more), tooltips, IME, and the app-wide AdornerLayer focus adorner
                              Control.OnGotFocus falls back to when a template declares no
                              FocusAdorner of its own. Checkboxes, scroll bars, toggle switches,
                              lists and buttons left this list in Task 3; the two text boxes, the
                              每列个数 NumericUpDown and the ButtonSpinner inside it left it in Task 4;
                              the two progress bars in Task 5; the three 外观 ComboBoxes and their
                              drop-down rows in Task 6; the 设置 page's six group boxes in Task 9.
                              Avalonia's own theme is Fluent 1 vocabulary, which layers 2 and 3
                              re-proportion.
      2. Aero/AeroResources — the vendored Windows 7 Aero layer. Task 2 put in the colours: 168
                              <Color> keys per variant and the 110-key brush dictionary built from
                              them, plus the metrics, referenced by nothing, so that a mistake in it
                              would surface as a build error rather than as a silently odd-looking
                              app. Tasks 3 to 6 and Task 9 spent them: fourteen control-theme
                              dictionaries are merged in now, plus one StyleInclude for the AVMAP
                              style sheet. 64 keys, of which 33 are not Aero-prefixed — fifteen
                              {x:Type X} ControlTheme keys, which is how a ControlTheme auto-applies
                              at all, the one opt-in "GroupBox" key Task 9's group boxes name
                              explicitly, eleven ButtonSpinner geometry-and-converter keys, and six
                              Fluent key names redefined. That file is the authority on its own
                              contents, on exactly which controls those fifteen keys reach, and on
                              why it sits at this position in the list.
      3. Shell/Tokens       — TanMenu's design tokens, Light + Dark, plus the Aero caption brushes
                              MainWindow's replaced WindowDrawnDecorations template reads. Also
                              overrides the three FluentTheme keys this app redefines for Avalonia's
                              own templates: ControlCornerRadius, OverlayCornerRadius and
                              ContentControlThemeFontFamily, which pull the standard controls into
                              this app's proportions and typography. That seam works because this
                              file is listed AFTER FluentTheme below and Application.Styles is
                              searched last-first — Tokens.axaml is the authority on it and carries
                              the measurements; do not re-derive it here.
      4. Shell/Launcher     — the launcher's own dialect. Every rule is an x:Key'd ControlTheme or a
                              .tm-* class selector, so it is opt-in: what asks for it by class gets
                              it and nothing else does. Application.Styles reaches every window, so
                              "opt-in" is the whole mechanism rather than a scoping one — the
                              Options dialog and the About box (Round 8, Tasks 2 and 4) host the
                              settings cards and DO carry tm- classes, while Dialogs/MessageBoxWindow
                              asks for nothing and is therefore untouched by this entry.

    THE ORDER OF 2 AND 3 IS THE POINT, not incidental. Application.Styles is searched last-first
    (Avalonia.Styling.Styles.TryGetResource counts DOWN from Count-1), so a key defined in a LATER
    entry wins. Aero after Fluent means Aero beats Fluent; Tokens after Aero means this app's own
    overrides still beat Aero. ContentControlThemeFontFamily is the one that would bite: the
    upstream Aero metrics file set it to a bundled font with no CJK coverage, which would render
    this app's Chinese item names as tofu. That line is deleted on vendoring AND this ordering keeps
    Tokens' value on top of it.

    RequestedThemeVariant is deliberately NOT pinned here any more. The retro theme was a fixed
    light palette, so the old build had to force Light or the OS could put dark Fluent chrome around
    a light launcher. Both the token layer and the vendored Aero layer have both variants, so the
    variant is a user setting (general.appearance) applied at startup by Services/AppTheme.
  -->
  <Application.Styles>
    <FluentTheme />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Shell/Tokens.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml" />
  </Application.Styles>

</Application>
